I don’t have the “data from 16,” so I can’t compute r. Please provide the paired data or the summary totals (n, Σx, Σy, Σx², Σy², Σxy). Then I’ll compute
r = [nΣxy − (Σx)(Σy)] / sqrt{[nΣx² − (Σx)²][nΣy² − (Σy)²]}
and select the correct option (rounded to three decimals).

The false positive rate is the probability that the test is positive given that the person does **not** have bowel cancer:
\[
\text{FPR} = P(\text{Test } + \mid \text{Cancer } N)
= \frac{\text{False positives}}{\text{All non-cases}}
= \frac{18}{18 + 182}
= \frac{18}{200}
= 0.09.
\]
So the false positive rate is **0.09 (9%)**.

\frac{2}{20} = \frac{1}{10}
**Explanation:**  
P(Cancer | Positive test) = \frac{\# \text{ true positives}}{\# \text{ total positives}} = \frac{2}{2+18} = \frac{2}{20} = \frac{1}{10}.
